"A control-flow graph represents possible transfers of execution between basic blocks within a program region.\n\nUse when: Inspect branches, loops, and unreachable code. Explain a compiler optimization or test-path requirement.\n\nAvoid when: Interprocedural calls or data provenance is the primary question.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote basic blocks containing straight-line instructions. Directed edges represent possible control transfers. Branch labels identify guards; special nodes mark entry and relevant exits.\n\nBuild: Choose one function or bounded region at a stated revision. Split instructions into basic blocks at transfer points. Add guarded branch, loop-back, return, and exceptional edges that apply. Place the main path vertically and send loop-back edges through exterior gutters.\n\nCheck: Each terminator has all its possible successors. A decision’s outgoing guards match the actual conditions. Exceptional exits are included or explicitly excluded. Back edges, guard labels, and block text do not intersect.\n\nPitfalls: Using line-by-line nodes that obscure basic-block structure. Assuming a visible graph path is feasible without checking conditions.\n\nExample: A validation function branches to Reject or continues to Save, with a retry loop returning to Validate.\n\nAlternatives: Call graph, Program dependence graph."
